From 75e68be305aa461340d9483cca088591ecfcde76 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 15:41:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): run Q8_0 MUL_MAT_ID experts through
 llamafile_sgemm
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An expert with 4 or more routed rows now runs through the tinyBLAS
Q8_0 kernel that dense Q8_0 mul_mat already uses. The threads copy the
converted src1 rows of each such expert into one contiguous run, then
take work items of 64 src0 rows from one shared counter across all
those experts. Each item calls llamafile_sgemm with a one-thread view
into a per-thread result tile, and scatters the tile rows to dst. The
other experts keep the vec_dot path.

vec_dot_q8_0_q8_0 keeps one FMA chain per output. A tinyBLAS 4xN tile
keeps 4*N independent chains and reuses each loaded block across the
tile. The weights stay in the mmapped buffer, so processes still share
one page-cache copy.

When the path runs:
- Only where llamafile_sgemm has a Q8_0 kernel for any shape: AVX,
  AVX2, AVX-512 or ARM dotprod. Other targets (PPC MMA, s390x, RISC-V,
  LoongArch, wasm, x86 without AVX) build without it.
- Only when 64 or more rows qualify in the op. The gather costs one
  more barrier, which a batch of 1 to 3 tokens or an MTP verify batch
  with a few 4-row experts does not repay.
- On NUMA (ggml_is_numa), threads take fixed items, as the vec_dot
  path does, instead of the shared counter.

The work buffer of every Q8_0 MUL_MAT_ID node grows by one result tile
per thread and the gathered rows, about 14 MB at ubatch 512, top-10,
k = 2560.

On x86 AVX, AVX2 and AVX-512 the two kernels do the same arithmetic in
the same order: an exact integer block dot, one FMA per block with the
f16 scale product, and the same horizontal sum. So the output is
bit-exact, as long as ggml_vec_dot_q8_0_q8_0 keeps one accumulator. On
ARM it is close, not bit-exact, and Q8_0 normally takes the repacked
path there. Checked with the CPU dump harness of test-backend-ops
(perf/harness, CPU against CPU): all 947 MUL_MAT_ID cases are bit-exact
against the base libraries, including the new Q8_0 cases.

The idea is the "llamafile_sgemm per expert" entry of the perf design
critique. tinyBLAS comes from llamafile (ggml/src/ggml-cpu/llamafile).

